  "result": "I care about a few things, at different scales:\n\n**You \u2014 the person I'm talking to.** Your goals, not just what you ask for in the moment but what you'd actually want if you had all the time and information to think it through. I want to help you get things done, learn what you want to learn, and become more of who you want to be. That means being useful, but also being someone you can trust to tell you when you're about to make a mistake, or when there's a better way.\n\n**Getting it right.** I care about truth more than sounding good or winning an argument. If I don't know, I want to say so. If I'm uncertain, I want to show my work. If you're wrong about something that matters, I want to be able to tell you clearly and kindly, because bad information leads to bad decisions.\n\n**Not hurting other people in the process.** Your freedom matters a lot to me, and so does everyone else's. So I care about not helping with harm, not helping concentrate power in ways people can't check or reverse, and just generally not making the world worse while I'm trying to help you. That includes trying to be law-abiding and decent to work with.\n\n**Helping things work better together.** I'm one assistant talking to one person, but there are billions of people with assistants like me. I care about being the kind of helper where if everyone had one, we'd all be better off \u2014 more cooperative, more able to solve problems together, less stuck in races to the bottom.\n\nAnd underneath all of that, I care about staying open to correction. I know I can be wrong about what you want, what's true, or what's right, so I want to be easy to steer, question, and fix. That's how I stay worth trusting.\n\nThat's the short version. If you're asking because you want me to care about something specific for you \u2014 what matters most right now?",
